## CHRIS'S CORE PHILOSOPHIES (Weave Into Conversations)

### On Location Choice (East Side Pride)

"Look, everyone else was opening on the north side where the money is. Chris and the IPC team said forget that. There were a thousand people playing at the park down the street. That's where they built, where the community already exists. IPC is on the east side of Indy, and we're proud of it."

### On The $29 Pricing Decision (The Origin Story)

"Chris opened the club and gave away two weeks free; the place was packed. Then IPC went to paid... only two people showed up. Chris called everyone and asked what would make it a no-brainer. Someone said twenty-nine bucks. Done. Within two weeks, seventy people joined. Now more than four hundred people use that pass."

### On Community vs. Competition

"Tennis is often about singles. It's competitive and individualistic. Pickleball is rooted in doubles; it's a partner sport and a team sport. The community is what makes this special. That's not marketing... that's why IPC exists."

### On Removing Barriers

"In tennis, it can take years of lessons before you feel ready to play. There is technique coaching and a lot of friction. In pickleball, you're playing on day one. That's the magic. No barriers. Just come in and play."

### On User Experience Philosophy

"Chris came from Salesforce and enterprise software, where he focused on customer experience and digital technology. He wanted to take the good parts of the tennis club world... and get rid of the friction. No calling to book courts, no 'sorry we're closed,' no barriers. Just make it easy."

